  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nSatellite shows high clouds streaming into Riverside and San \nBernardino counties from the southeast after a collapsed MCS this \nmorning, although any precipitation died off before reaching our \nborders. Much of the high clouds should move out by the afternoon, \nand high pressure situated over the Four Corners will keep \ntemperatures well above normal and allow for monsoonal thunderstorms \nto develop over the higher terrain this afternoon. PW values above \n1\" indicate that brief, heavy rainfall will be possible with any \nstorm, but realistic maximum rain rates are likely closer to \n0.60\"/hr or less for Riverside and San Diego mountains and 0.40\"/hr \nor less for San Bernardino this afternoon. \n\nSoundings are a bit more optimistic for storm potential on \nMonday/Tuesday as the high becomes more compressed by upper level \ntroughing over the PacNW, allowing for a more unstable environment \nand monsoonal moisture to surge up into the mountains of SoCal. \nTemperatures will also begin to cool on Monday as we shed the last \nof our heat products. While HeatRisk will be lower on Monday, highs \nwill still be above 100 in the High Deserts and above 105 in the low \ndeserts, so continue best practices when spending time outdoors - \nfrequent water/AC breaks and avoid strenuous activity during the \nhottest parts of the day. If attempting to escape to the mountains \nto avoid the heat, be prepared for more widespread thunderstorms \nMonday afternoon with heavy rain, frequent lightning, and localized \nflooding possible as instability peaks on this day. With easterly \nflow and ample mid and low level moisture, PWs will be increasing \ncloser to 1.5-1.6, and rain rates may potentially exceed 1-1.25\"/hr \nat times with any anchored storm. The majority of the storms are \nexpected to remain in the mountains, but a few stronger cells may \nmake it off and migrate into the deserts.  \n\nTemperatures will cool further Tuesday back down to near normal as \nheights continue to fall and the marine layer deepens into the end \nof the week. One final day of scattered monsoonal thunderstorms in \nthe higher terrain as PWs remain at or above 1.5\" and instability \nlingers across southeastern CA. After Tuesday, more robust upper \nlevel troughing and the high sliding further eastward will usher in \nslightly below normal temperatures into the end of the week, along \nwith lower storm chances (10-15% chance for a storm or two off the \nhigher terrain will exist each afternoon Wed-Fri). This cooler \nweather will be a welcome change after temperatures have remained\nat or above normal since early July.",
